        ]
      },
      "extent": {
        "metaparameter": "n_nrgrowthpatternfinder"
      },
      "metadata": {
        "tags": [
          "population",
          "growth"
        ],
        "note": "4,189 rows, and no decimal column -- SCC is in float_columns because it is a numeric KEY stored as utf8, not a measurement. J8, and the harder half of it is not the join: the SCC walks the equipment chain [SCC, SCC[0..7]+000, SCC[0..4]+000000] and a stateID 26 row beats a stateID 0 one, both of which components/growth_index.esm precomputes into effective key columns.",
        "esio_format": "parquet"
      }
    },
    "nr_logging_county_nrgrowthindex": {
      "kind": "static",
      "source": {
        "url_template": "file://${MOVES_SNAPSHOTS}/nr-logging-county/tables/db__movesexecution1ccc0232_campuscluster_illinois_edu__nrgrowthindex.parquet"
      },
      "reader_options": {
        "float_columns": [
          "growthIndex"
        ]
      },
      "extent": {
        "metaparameter": "n_nrgrowthindex"
      },
      "metadata": {
        "tags": [
          "population",
          "growth"
        ],
        "note": "50,955 rows. J9. growthIndex is int64 in this snapshot and is nonetheless listed in float_columns, because 43 of its rows are NULL (pattern 1169 has no index at all) and the runtime refuses an integer column with a missing value rather than substituting one -- read as float64 those rows arrive as NaN, and the (pattern, year) join never reaches them. Pattern 2176, the one this fixture reads, has no nulls. The document still models the truncation toward zero explicitly (nonroad_loader.rs:1516), because it is canonical's %20d packet write and not a decode artefact, and because the year-over-year changes sit near zero so the truncation decides the SIGN of the growth factor.",
        "esio_format": "parquet"
      }
    },
