        },
        {
          "id": "the_carried_age_distribution_sums_to_the_growth_ratio",
          "description": "The check that keeps the ONE carried column honest. `age_grownModelYearFraction` is still data -- not because the fold has no spelling any more (it has: esm-spec §4.3.1.1, and components/age_distribution.esm computes it) but because this document ingests, and a document that ingests is forced onto an evaluation path that resolves a causal self-read to zero without a diagnostic (finding F24). A carried column nobody is testing is not a number, it is an assertion. Its sum must be the cumulative growth G(2020)/G(1990), which this document derives from nrgrowthindex through a completely different route: the pattern finder's ladder, then two exact rows of a 50,955-row table. 4.6978168 against 4.6978192, agreeing to 5.2e-07 -- which is where two float32-printed fractions summed in float32 should land, and far tighter than any modelling error would be. That 5.2e-07 is four epsilons of the declared element type and is the one number in this document that needs a tolerance looser than one: the gap is in the CARRIED fractions, not in the summing, so it does not tighten with precision.",
          "time_span": { "start": 0.0, "end": 0.0 },
          "tolerance": { "rel": 1e-6 },
          "assertions": [
            { "variable": "run_baseGrowthIndex", "time": 0.0, "expected": 321.0, "coords": { "run_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "run_endGrowthIndex", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1508.0, "coords": { "run_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "run_cumulativeGrowthRatio", "time": 0.0, "expected": 4.697819314641745, "coords": { "run_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "age_grownModelYearFractionTotal", "time": 0.0, "expected": 4.697819314641745, "coords": { "run_rows": 1 } }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_activity_point_and_its_deterioration_ages",
          "description": "§6.1's inputs, out of 1,183 nrsourceusetype rows: hpAvg 6.81, 303 hours a year, load factor 0.7, median life 191 full-load hours; cvttmp = 6.81 x 0.7 = 4.767 from unitcf.f's g/hp-hr branch; and detage = (idxyr+1) x 303 x 0.7 / 191 for the three cohorts. All three detages exceed this technology's emissionCap of 1, so §4.1's cap-inside-the-power rule makes every deterioration factor 1 + A here -- which is why §6.2's B = 0.5, cap = 2 point is the one that tests the rule properly, in components/deteriorated_emission_rate.esm.\n\nBINARY32 QUANTITIES: every number this test pins is a floating-point quantity, so it asserts at one binary32 epsilon (2^-23) rather than the model's 1e-12 -- the resolution of the declared element type. The tests that pin a ten-digit key keep the exact default; the precision note in `metadata.description` says why a key must never be compared within a tolerance.",
          "time_span": { "start": 0.0, "end": 0.0 },
          "tolerance": { "rel": 1.1920929e-7 },
          "assertions": [
            { "variable": "run_hpAvg", "time": 0.0, "expected": 6.81, "coords": { "run_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "run_hoursUsedPerYear", "time": 0.0, "expected": 303.0, "coords": { "run_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "run_loadFactor", "time": 0.0, "expected": 0.7, "coords": { "run_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "run_medianLifeFullLoad", "time": 0.0, "expected": 191.0, "coords": { "run_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "run_unitConversion", "time": 0.0, "expected": 4.766999999999999, "coords": { "run_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "age_detAge", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1.1104712041884817, "coords": { "age_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "age_detAge", "time": 0.0, "expected": 2.2209424083769633, "coords": { "age_rows": 2 } },
            { "variable": "age_detAge", "time": 0.0, "expected": 3.331413612565445, "coords": { "age_rows": 3 } }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "every_cohort_takes_the_2008_technology_mix",
          "description": "§6.1: the bin carries ten distinct mix years (1900, 1996, 1997, 2002-2008) and the latest at or before every one of 2020, 2019 and 2018 is 2008, where engTechID 125 holds the whole fraction and the other five techs hold 0.00. Taking the LARGEST mix year rather than the latest at or before would give the same answer here and the wrong one for a 1990s cohort; taking the first row would give 1900.",
          "time_span": { "start": 0.0, "end": 0.0 },
          "assertions": [
            { "variable": "age_mixYearID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 2008.0, "coords": { "age_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "age_mixYearID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 2008.0, "coords": { "age_rows": 3 } },
            { "variable": "age_modelYearID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 2020.0, "coords": { "age_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "age_modelYearID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 2018.0, "coords": { "age_rows": 3 } },
            { "variable": "age_isPopulated", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1.0, "coords": { "age_rows": 3 } }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_technology_mix_sums_to_one_for_every_cohort",
          "description": "The check that the 100-199 engine-technology window is a SUPERSET of the codes the data carries, and that the hp bin and the exhaust process group each admitted exactly one row set. A code outside the window would make this less than 1; a second hp bin joining in would make it 2; the evap rows, which bin hp differently, would do the same. Six technologies share this bin at mix year 2008 and engTechID 125 holds the whole fraction.\n\nBINARY32 QUANTITIES: every number this test pins is a floating-point quantity, so it asserts at one binary32 epsilon (2^-23) rather than the model's 1e-12 -- the resolution of the declared element type. The tests that pin a ten-digit key keep the exact default; the precision note in `metadata.description` says why a key must never be compared within a tolerance.",
