        },
        {
          "id": "the_twelve_rows_of_the_worked_example_end_to_end",
          "description": "docs/nonroad-logging-county.md §6.1's results table, recomputed here from seventeen snapshot tables and no transcribed input except agedist.f's three grown fractions (F12). Every value below is this document's binary64 answer; the specification's independently written float32 reproduction prints 269.1395, 1205.139, 6.923390, 44.16037, 71.91162, 322.0022, 1.849867, 11.79925, 4.274964e-06, 1.914222e-05, 1.099699e-07 and 7.014354e-07 for the same twelve cells, and the snapshot's own MOVESOutput agrees with these to 4.0e-06 relative -- which is where its six-significant-figure storage puts it. Those printed digits are not the `expected` here and neither is this document's own output: the binary64 values are kept, and the document -- now evaluated in float32 -- reproduces every one of them to between 3.9e-09 and 1.13e-07, inside the one binary32 epsilon this test asserts at. So the twelve cells are pinned to seven digits of a number derived from seventeen tables, by a route that is independent of both the specification's reproduction and the snapshot.\n\nThe three-order spread across model years is the age distribution: MY2018's grown fraction is 5.9e-08 against MY2020's 3.7, and it is the cohort that the `modfrc <= 0` skip drops when agedist.f's thirty-year fold is EXECUTED in binary64 instead of float32 (PLAN.md §1.6.1). This document does not execute that fold -- F12 -- so the skip is not what keeps MY2018 here and the element type is not what recovers it: `age_grownModelYearFraction` carries the float32 fold's OUTPUT as data, `5.8885583e-08`, and `age_isPopulated` reads it as positive in either precision. All twelve rows were present before this document declared Float32 and are present after. `run-oracle.sh --float64`, which does execute the fold, drops these four cells and emits 140 rows; the two facts are consistent, and the difference between them is F12, not precision.\n\nBINARY32 QUANTITIES: every number this test pins is a floating-point quantity, so it asserts at one binary32 epsilon (2^-23) rather than the model's 1e-12 -- the resolution of the declared element type. The tests that pin a ten-digit key keep the exact default; the precision note in `metadata.description` says why a key must never be compared within a tolerance.",
          "time_span": { "start": 0.0, "end": 0.0 },
          "tolerance": { "rel": 1.1920929e-7 },
          "assertions": [
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 269.1395238307408, "coords": { "output_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1205.1395403479848, "coords": { "output_rows": 2 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 6.923390114994543, "coords": { "output_rows": 3 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 44.16036911057204, "coords": { "output_rows": 4 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 71.9116238258302, "coords": { "output_rows": 5 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 322.00228360974603, "coords": { "output_rows": 6 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1.84986663594629, "coords": { "output_rows": 7 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 11.799247491744838, "coords": { "output_rows": 8 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 4.2749635666031645e-06, "coords": { "output_rows": 9 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1.914221870623698e-05, "coords": { "output_rows": 10 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1.0996987762226844e-07, "coords": { "output_rows": 11 } },
            { "variable": "out_emissionQuant", "time": 0.0, "expected": 7.014353237622864e-07, "coords": { "output_rows": 12 } }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_output_relation_carries_the_moves_key_as_columns",
          "description": "Twelve rows over one axis, three model years crossed with four pollutant processes, and the key spelled as columns rather than as axes -- because the fixture's key set is ragged across SCCs (3, 4 and 29 model years) and a `ragged` axis reads only its offsets (docs/findings F14). The packed polProcessID is unpacked into the two columns MOVESOutput actually carries.",
          "time_span": { "start": 0.0, "end": 0.0 },
